-- pending: capabilities in tasks (a task given main's stdout; decision 57's ports, SPEC §30 Open)
-- now: 41:19: the body of `with spawn` assigns a `let mut` variable declared outside it, or lends a place declared outside it: a control handler's body may be abandoned, and does not hand them back; bind what the block answers instead
-- Decision 71 permits this shape: each task owns the stream across its `await`s and hands it back.
-- What blocks it is two tasks given one capability (SPEC §34 *Open*), not the rule.
-- Tasks that print their progress. Each download task is given the output stream, writes a line
-- per chunk it fetches, and awaits between chunks, so the lines of the two tasks interleave. The
-- task takes the stream and hands it back with its value, as any function that writes does.
--
-- Exercises: a capability (`stdout`) passed to a task and held across its `await`s; output from
-- interleaved tasks; `spawn` and `run_until_empty`.
--
-- Blocked by: a capability in a task. `stdout` is `main`'s, and a `with spawn { … }` whose body
-- lends it is rejected: "the body of `with spawn` … lends a place declared outside it: a control
-- handler's body may be abandoned, and does not hand them back". A capability has no destructor,
-- so a task that holds one cannot be cancelled (`lib/yield.bc`'s note: a generator cannot hold
-- one across a `yield`), and two tasks cannot both be given it. A task that writes through
-- `Stdout` (SPEC §34) is given a borrow of the stream instead, which it can drop when cancelled
-- (`corpus/io-task.bc`), but a borrow is lent to one task at a time: two writers wait on SPEC §34
-- *Open* (concurrent writers; `corpus/pending/io-two-tasks.bc`).
--
-- Once it lands: prints `1:0`, `2:0`, `1:1`, `2:1`, `1:2`, each on a line, and answers `i32[0]`.

include async
include textio

fn download(o: Out, id: i32, chunks: i32) -> Out * i32 with Async =
  let mut o = o;
  for i in 0..chunks {
    put_dec(&mut o, id);
    putb(&mut o, b':');
    put_dec(&mut o, i);
    putb(&mut o, b'\n');
    await()
  };
  (o, chunks)

-- Both tasks write to standard output, one after the other in time.
main -> i32 =
  let q = queue[Out * i32]();
  let q = push(q, with spawn { download(stdout, 1, 3) });
  let q = push(q, with spawn { download(stdout, 2, 2) });
  let done = run_until_empty(q);
  0
